在 C# 中使用 `Client.Send` 方法发送数据的步骤如下：

1. 创建一个 socket 客户端，与服务端建立连接。

```
Socket clientSocket = new Socket(AddressFamily.InterNetwork, SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);
IPAddress serverIP = IPAddress.Parse("192.168.1.100"); // 服务端的 IP 地址
int serverPort = 12345; // 服务端的端口号
clientSocket.Connect(new IPEndPoint(serverIP, serverPort));
```

2. 准备要发送的数据，将数据转换成 byte[] 类型的数组。

```
string message = "Hello World!";
byte[] sendData = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(message);
```

3. 调用 `Client.Send` 方法发送数据。

```
int bytesSent = clientSocket.Send(sendData, 0, sendData.Length, SocketFlags.None);
```

其中，`bytesSent` 变量表示发送的字节数。

4. 关闭 socket 连接。

```
clientSocket.Close();
```

完整的示例代码：

```
using System;
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Sockets;
using System.Text;

namespace SocketClient
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            Socket clientSocket = new Socket(AddressFamily.InterNetwork, SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);
            IPAddress serverIP = IPAddress.Parse("192.168.1.100"); // 服务端的 IP 地址
            int serverPort = 12345; // 服务端的端口号
            clientSocket.Connect(new IPEndPoint(serverIP, serverPort));

            string message = "Hello World!";
            byte[] sendData = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(message);

            int bytesSent = clientSocket.Send(sendData, 0, sendData.Length, SocketFlags.None);
            Console.WriteLine($"Sent {bytesSent} bytes to server");

            clientSocket.Close();
        }
    }
}
``` 

注意：在发送数据之前，一定要先与服务端建立连接。发送数据完成后，还需要关闭 socket 连接以释放资源。

在 C# 中，BinaryFormatter 是一个类，用于在网络上传输和存储对象的二进制数据，可以将对象序列化为二进制流，或者将二进制流反序列化为对象。

使用 BinaryFormatter 需要注意以下几点：

1. 要使用 BinaryFormatter，需要在代码中引用 System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary 命名空间。

2. 要将某个对象序列化为二进制流，需要创建一个 MemoryStream 对象，并将该对象传递给 BinaryFormatter 对象的 Serialize 方法。Serialize 方法会将对象序列化并存储到 MemoryStream 中。

```
using System.IO;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;

MemoryStream memoryStream = new MemoryStream();
BinaryFormatter binaryFormatter = new BinaryFormatter();
binaryFormatter.Serialize(memoryStream, yourObject);
byte[] binaryData = memoryStream.ToArray();
```

3. 要将从网络中接收的二进制数据转化为对象，需要创建一个 MemoryStream 对象，并将二进制数据写入该对象。然后，将 MemoryStream 对象传递给 BinaryFormatter 对象的 Deserialize 方法。Deserialize 方法会将二进制数据反序列化并转换为对象。

```
using System.IO;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;

MemoryStream memoryStream = new MemoryStream(binaryData);
BinaryFormatter binaryFormatter = new BinaryFormatter();
object yourObject = binaryFormatter.Deserialize(memoryStream);
```

注意：使用 BinaryFormatter 进行对象序列化和反序列化时，对象所属的类必须具有 [Serializable] 特性。否则在序列化时会抛出 SerializationException 异常。

完整的示例代码：

```
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Net.Sockets;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;

namespace SocketClient
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            Socket clientSocket = new Socket(AddressFamily.InterNetwork, SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);
            clientSocket.Connect("127.0.0.1", 12345);

            Person person = new Person() { Name = "Alice", Age = 18 };
            MemoryStream memoryStream = new MemoryStream();
            BinaryFormatter binaryFormatter = new BinaryFormatter();
            binaryFormatter.Serialize(memoryStream, person);
            byte[] sendData = memoryStream.ToArray();

            clientSocket.Send(sendData);

            byte[] receiveData = new byte[1024];
            int bytesReceived = clientSocket.Receive(receiveData);
            MemoryStream receiveMemoryStream = new MemoryStream(receiveData);
            Person receivedPerson = (Person)binaryFormatter.Deserialize(receiveMemoryStream);
            Console.WriteLine($"Received {receivedPerson.Name}, {receivedPerson.Age} years old");

            clientSocket.Close();
        }
    }

    [Serializable]
    class Person
    {
        public string Name { get; set; }
        public int Age { get; set; }
    }
}
```

在这个例子中，我们使用 BinaryFormatter 将一个 Person 对象序列化为二进制数据，并将其发送到服务器。然后，我们从服务器接收二进制数据，并将其反序列化为 Person 对象，最后输出 Person 对象的姓名和年龄。注意，Person 对象必须标记为可序列化的，才能使用 BinaryFormatter 序列化和反序列化该对象。

To register Keycloak authorization server in Spring, you need to follow the below steps:

1. Firstly, add the Keycloak Spring Boot starter dependency in your project's build.gradle or pom.xml file. This can be done by adding the following code in the dependency tag:

For Gradle:

```
dependencies {
    compile group: 'org.keycloak', name: 'keycloak-spring-boot-starter', version: '15.0.2'
}
```

For Maven:

```
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.keycloak</groupId>
    <artifactId>keycloak-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
    <version>15.0.2</version>
</dependency>
```

2. Next, you need to configure Keycloak in your Spring Boot application by specifying the Keycloak server details in the application.properties file. Here's an example:

```
keycloak.auth-server-url=http://localhost:8080/auth
keycloak.realm=myrealm
keycloak.resource=myclient
keycloak.credentials.secret=mysecret
keycloak.ssl-required=external
keycloak.use-resource-role-mappings=true
```

3. Now, you should enable the Keycloak adapter in your Spring Boot application by annotating your main application class with the @EnableWebSecurity and @KeycloakConfiguration annotations. Here's an example:

```
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableWebSecurity
@KeycloakConfiguration
public class MyAppApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MyAppApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

4. Finally, you need to configure the security settings specific to your application by extending the KeycloakWebSecurityConfigurerAdapter class and overriding its methods as per your requirements. Here's an example:

```
@KeycloakConfiguration
public class SecurityConfig extends KeycloakWebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {

    @Autowired
    public void configureGlobal(AuthenticationManagerBuilder auth) {
        KeycloakAuthenticationProvider keycloakAuthenticationProvider = keycloakAuthenticationProvider();
        auth.authenticationProvider(keycloakAuthenticationProvider);
    }

    @Override
    protected void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        super.configure(http);
        http.authorizeRequests()
                .antMatchers("/myprotected/**").hasRole("user")
                .anyRequest().permitAll();
    }
}
```

These steps should help you register Keycloak authorization server in your Spring Boot application.

Keycloak is a popular open-source identity and access management solution that can be integrated into Spring Boot applications for secure authentication and authorization. To use Keycloak in your Spring Boot project, you need to follow the steps below:

1. Add Keycloak dependencies: Add the Keycloak Spring Boot starter dependency in your project's build.gradle or pom.xml file.

For Gradle:

```
dependencies {
    compile group: 'org.keycloak', name: 'keycloak-spring-boot-starter', version: '15.0.2'
}
```

For Maven:

```
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.keycloak</groupId>
    <artifactId>keycloak-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
    <version>15.0.2</version>
</dependency>
```

2. Configure Keycloak properties: Configure Keycloak properties in the application.properties or application.yml file. Here is an example:

```
# Keycloak properties
keycloak.auth-server-url=http://localhost:8080/auth
keycloak.realm=myrealm
keycloak.resource=myclient
keycloak.credentials.secret=mysecret
keycloak.ssl-required=external
keycloak.use-resource-role-mappings=true
keycloak.security-constraints[0].authRoles[0]=user
keycloak.security-constraints[0].securityCollections[0].patterns[0]=/myprotected/*
```

3. Enable and configure Keycloak within your application: Enable Keycloak in your main Spring Boot class by adding the following annotations: @EnableWebSecurity, @EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true) and @KeycloakConfiguration

```
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true)
@KeycloakConfiguration
public class KeycloakApplication extends KeycloakWebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {

    /**
     * Registers the KeycloakAuthenticationProvider with the authentication manager.
     */
    @Autowired
    public void configureGlobal(AuthenticationManagerBuilder auth) throws Exception {
        KeycloakAuthenticationProvider keycloakAuthProvider = keycloakAuthenticationProvider();
        auth.authenticationProvider(keycloakAuthProvider);
    }

    /**
     * Defines the session authentication strategy.
     */
    @Bean
    @Override
    protected SessionAuthenticationStrategy sessionAuthenticationStrategy() {
        return new RegisterSessionAuthenticationStrategy(new SessionRegistryImpl());
    }

    /**
     * Configures the HttpSecurity, specifying URL patterns and roles that are allowed
     * to access these URL patterns.
     */
    @Override
    protected void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        super.configure(http);
        http
                .authorizeRequests()
                .antMatchers("/myprotected/**").hasRole("user")
                .anyRequest().permitAll();
    }
}
```

4. Use Keycloak for Application Security: You can use Keycloak for application security by using the KeycloakAuthenticationToken in your application's controllers or services.

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/users")
public class UserController {

    @GetMapping
    public List<User> getAllUsers(Authentication authentication) {
        KeycloakAuthenticationToken authenticationToken = (KeycloakAuthenticationToken) authentication;
        AccessToken accessToken = authenticationToken.getAccount().getKeycloakSecurityContext().getToken();
        // use accessToken to perform authorization or extract user information
    }

    @PostMapping
    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('admin')")
    public User createUser(@RequestBody User user) {
        // create user
    }
}
```

These are the basic steps you need to follow to use Keycloak provider in Spring Boot. Keycloak acts as the identity provider and authenticates the users for your application, which results in a more secure application.

To authenticate with an Azure tenancy using Microsoft.Identity and a registered app secret, you can follow these steps:

1. Register your application in the Azure Active Directory (AAD) portal, and get its application ID (clientId) and client secret.

2. Install the required NuGet packages:
- Microsoft.Identity.Client
- Microsoft.Graph

3. Add the following class (AuthenticationProvider.cs) to your project:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Graph;
using Microsoft.Identity.Client;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace MyOutlookPlugin
{
    public class AuthenticationProvider : IAuthenticationProvider
    {
        private readonly string _clientId;
        private readonly string _clientSecret;
        private readonly string _tenantId;
        private readonly string[] _scopes;

        public AuthenticationProvider(string clientId, string tenantId, string clientSecret, string[] scopes)
        {
            _clientId = clientId;
            _tenantId = tenantId;
            _clientSecret = clientSecret;
            _scopes = scopes;
        }

        public async Task<string> GetAccessTokenAsync()
        {
            var app = ConfidentialClientApplicationBuilder
                .Create(_clientId)
                .WithTenantId(_tenantId)
                .WithClientSecret(_clientSecret)
                .Build();

            var result = await app.AcquireTokenForClient(_scopes).ExecuteAsync();

            return result.AccessToken;
        }

        public async Task AuthenticateRequestAsync(HttpRequestMessage requestMessage)
        {
            var accessToken = await GetAccessTokenAsync();
            requestMessage.Headers.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", accessToken);
        }
    }
}
```

4. Update your App.config or App.xaml.cs file to have your app settings:

```xml
<appSettings>
  <add key="ClientId" value="your_client_id"/>
  <add key="TenantId" value="your_tenant_id"/>
  <add key="ClientSecret" value="your_client_secret"/>
  <add key="Scopes" value="https://graph.microsoft.com/.default"/>
</appSettings>
```

5. Instantiate the `AuthenticationProvider` and Microsoft Graph `GraphServiceClient` in your main class:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Graph;
using System.Configuration;

namespace MyOutlookPlugin
{
    public class MainClass
    {
        private static GraphServiceClient _graphClient;

        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Read settings from the config file
            var clientId = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["ClientId"];
            var tenantId = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["TenantId"];
            var clientSecret = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["ClientSecret"];
            var scopes = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["Scopes"].Split(';');

            var authenticationProvider = new AuthenticationProvider(clientId, tenantId, clientSecret, scopes);
            _graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(authenticationProvider);

            // Now you can use the _graphClient object to interact with the Microsoft Graph API.
        }
    }
}
```

Now you must have a complete and working integration with Microsoft Graph using your registered app secret. The `GraphServiceClient` object can be used to access different services such as users, mails, calendars, etc. For more information, check out the [Microsoft Graph documentation](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/overview).

To request the appropriate scopes for editing a calendar, you can update the "Scopes" setting in your App.config file as follows:

```xml
<appSettings>
  <add key="ClientId" value="your_client_id"/>
  <add key="TenantId" value="your_tenant_id"/>
  <add key="ClientSecret" value="your_client_secret"/>
  <add key="Scopes" value="https://graph.microsoft.com/Calendars.ReadWrite"/>
</appSettings>
```

Note: Make sure to grant the required Calendar permissions to your app in the Azure Active Directory (AAD) portal. These permissions include:

- Calendars.ReadWrite: Allows the app to create, read, update, and delete events in all calendars that the user has access to.
- Calendars.ReadWrite.Shared: Allows the app to create, read, update, and delete events in all calendars the user has access to, and any shared with the user.

Now, you can use the Microsoft.Graph.Calendar API to interact with calendars. For example, you can create an event in the user's calendar as shown below:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Graph;
using System;
using System.Configuration;

namespace MyOutlookPlugin
{
    public class MainClass
    {
        private static GraphServiceClient _graphClient;

        static async void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Read settings from the config file
            var clientId = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["ClientId"];
            var tenantId = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["TenantId"];
            var clientSecret = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["ClientSecret"];
            var scopes = ConfigurationManager.AppSettings["Scopes"].Split(';');

            var authenticationProvider = new AuthenticationProvider(clientId, tenantId, clientSecret, scopes);
            _graphClient = new GraphServiceClient(authenticationProvider);

            // Now you can use the _graphClient object to interact with the Microsoft Graph API.
            await CreateEvent();
        }

        private static async Task CreateEvent()
        {
            var newEvent = new Event
            {
                Subject = "Test Event",
                Start = new DateTimeTimeZone
                {
                    DateTime = DateTime.UtcNow.AddHours(1).ToString("o"),
                    TimeZone = "UTC"
                },
                End = new DateTimeTimeZone
                {
                    DateTime = DateTime.UtcNow.AddHours(2).ToString("o"),
                    TimeZone = "UTC"
                }
            };

            await _graphClient.Me.Events.Request().AddAsync(newEvent);
            Console.WriteLine("Event created.");
        }
    }
}
```

Now you have a complete and working integration with Microsoft Graph using the appropriate scopes for editing a calendar. The `GraphServiceClient` object can be used to access different calendar services such as creating, reading, updating, and deleting events. For more information, check out the [Microsoft Graph documentation](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/overview).

In MongoDB, you can perform multiple nested complex collections joins using the `$lookup` stage in an aggregation pipeline. You can create multiple pipelines with various conditions and join multiple collections in any combination. Here's an example that demonstrates joining multiple nested complex collections considering multiple pipeline conditions.

Consider the following collections:

1. orders
2. products
3. customers
4. shipping

Each order document contains `customerId`, `productId`, and `shippingId`. You want to fetch orders with their corresponding customer, product, and shipping information.

Here's an aggregation pipeline that demonstrates joining the collections:

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  // First $lookup stage - join with customers collection
  {
    $lookup: {
      from: "customers",
      localField: "customerId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "customer"
    }
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$customer"
  },
  // Second $lookup stage - join with products collection
  {
    $lookup: {
      from: "products",
      localField: "productId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "product"
    },
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$product"
  },
  // Third $lookup stage - join with shipping collection
  {
    $lookup: {
      from: "shipping",
      localField: "shippingId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "shipping"
    },
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$shipping"
  },
  // Additional pipeline stages for filtering or sorting if needed
  // {
  //   $match, $sort, $group, or other aggregations
  // },
])
```

In the above example, we used three `$lookup` stages to join the `orders` collection with `customers`, `products`, and `shipping` collections, and used the `$unwind` stage to deconstruct the arrays created by the `$lookup`.

You can also add additional aggregation stages like `$match`, `$sort`, `$group`, `$project`, etc., if needed, to customize the output or further filter the results based on specific conditions.

It's essential to be cautious with using too many `$lookup` stages or joining vast collections, as it may affect your system's performance. Always try to optimize your queries to fetch only the necessary data.

You can join multiple nested complex collections in MongoDB based on the `_id` field by using the `$lookup` stage in an aggregation pipeline. This example demonstrates joining multiple nested complex collections on the `_id` field while considering multiple pipeline conditions.

Consider the following collections:

1. orders
2. orderDetails
3. products
4. categories

The `orders` collection contains documents with order information; the `orderDetails` collection contains the product and quantity for each order, with an `orderId` field referencing the `orders` collection; the `products` collection has a `categoryId` field referencing the `categories` collection.

Now, you want to fetch order details with the corresponding order, product, and category information. You can use an aggregation pipeline to join these collections:

```javascript
db.orderDetails.aggregate([
  {
    // Join orderDetails with orders collection on orderDetails.orderId = orders._id
    $lookup: {
      from: "orders",
      localField: "orderId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "order"
    }
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$order"
  },
  {
    // Join orderDetails with products collection on orderDetails.productId = products._id
    $lookup: {
      from: "products",
      localField: "productId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "product"
    }
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$product"
  },
  {
    // Join products with categories collection on product.categoryId = categories._id
    $lookup: {
      from: "categories",
      localField: "product.categoryId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "category"
    }
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$category"
  },
  // Additional pipeline stages for filtering, sorting, or transformations if needed
])
```

In this example, we used the `$lookup` operator to join the orderDetails, orders, products, and categories collections based on their `_id` fields. We also used `$unwind` to deconstruct the arrays created by the `$lookup`.

You can add additional aggregation stages like `$match`, `$sort`, `$group`, `$project`, etc., if needed, to customize the output, filter the results based on specific conditions or transform the output in a more meaningful format.

Make sure to optimize your queries to fetch only the necessary data, as too many `$lookup` stages or joining massive collections may negatively impact your system's performance.

In MongoDB, you can join multiple nested complex collections and even perform a self-join using the `$lookup` stage in the aggregation pipeline. This example demonstrates joining multiple nested complex collections, including self-joins, while considering multiple pipeline conditions.

Consider the following collections:

1. orders
2. orderDetails
3. products
4. categories
5. employees

The `orders` collection has an `employeeId` field, referencing the `employees` collection. The `employees` collection also contains a `managerId` field, which refers to the `_id` of another employee (self-join).

Now, you want to fetch order details with the corresponding order, product, category, employee, and manager (employee) information. You can use the following aggregation pipeline:

```javascript
db.orderDetails.aggregate([
  {
    // Join orderDetails with orders collection on orderDetails.orderId = orders._id
    $lookup: {
      from: "orders",
      localField: "orderId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "order"
    }
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$order"
  },
  {
    // Join orderDetails with products collection on orderDetails.productId = products._id
    $lookup: {
      from: "products",
      localField: "productId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "product"
    }
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$product"
  },
  {
    // Join products with categories collection on product.categoryId = categories._id
    $lookup: {
      from: "categories",
      localField: "product.categoryId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "category"
    }
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$category"
  },
  {
    // Join orders with employees collection on orders.employeeId = employees._id
    $lookup: {
      from: "employees",
      localField: "order.employeeId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "employee"
    }
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$employee"
  },
  {
    // Self join - Join employees with their manager (also in employees collection) on employees.managerId = employees._id
    $lookup: {
      from: "employees",
      localField: "employee.managerId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "manager"
    }
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$manager"
  },
  // Additional pipeline stages for filtering, sorting, or transformations if needed
])
```

In this example, the `$lookup` operator is used to join the orderDetails, orders, products, categories, employees, and self-join manager (employee) collections based on their `_id` fields. The `$unwind` operator is used to deconstruct the arrays created by `$lookup`.

You can add additional aggregation stages like `$match`, `$sort`, `$group`, `$project`, etc., if needed, to customize the output, filter the results based on specific conditions, or transform the output in a more meaningful format.

Keep in mind that too many `$lookup` operators or joining large collections may negatively impact your system's performance. It is crucial to optimize your queries to fetch only the necessary data.

