import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class IndexFiles {

	public static void main(String[] args) {
		Map<String, String> fileIndex = new HashMap<>();
		File root = new File("C:\\");
		indexFiles(root, fileIndex);
		System.out.println("Indexed " + fileIndex.size() + " files.");
	}

	public static void indexFiles(File dir, Map<String, String> fileIndex) {
		File[] files = dir.listFiles();
		if (files != null) {
			for (File file : files) {
				if (file.isDirectory()) {
					indexFiles(file, fileIndex);
				} else {
					String filePath = file.getAbsolutePath();
					String fileName = file.getName();
					fileIndex.put(filePath, fileName);
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
```
In this code, we start by creating a `HashMap` named `fileIndex` that will store the file paths and their respective file names. Then, we create a `File` object for the C drive and pass it to the `indexFiles()` method, which we'll define next.

The `indexFiles()` method is a recursive method that takes a `File` object representing a directory and a `Map` to store the indexed files. If the current `File` object is a directory, we recursively call the `indexFiles()` method passing it as an argument. If the current `File` object is a file, we get its absolute path and name and add it to the `fileIndex` `Map`. Finally, we print the total number of files indexed.

Note that this code will take some time to execute because it is indexing all files in the C drive recursively. Additionally, this code does not handle errors or exceptions, so you'll want to add error handling to your final implementation.

To persist the `HashMap` of indexed files to later index updates, you can use Java serialization to write the `Map` object to a file on disk. Here's an updated version of the previous example that adds serialization support:

```
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class IndexFiles {

	public static void main(String[] args) {
		Map<String, String> fileIndex = loadIndex();
		// If the index is null, we need to create a new one
		if (fileIndex == null) {
			fileIndex = new HashMap<>();
			File root = new File("C:\\");
			indexFiles(root, fileIndex);
			saveIndex(fileIndex);
		}
		System.out.println("Indexed " + fileIndex.size() + " files.");
	}

	public static void indexFiles(File dir, Map<String, String> fileIndex) {
		File[] files = dir.listFiles();
		if (files != null) {
			for (File file : files) {
				if (file.isDirectory()) {
					indexFiles(file, fileIndex);
				} else {
					String filePath = file.getAbsolutePath();
					String fileName = file.getName();
					fileIndex.put(filePath, fileName);
				}
			}
		}
	}

	public static void saveIndex(Map<String, String> fileIndex) {
		try (ObjectOutputStream oos = new ObjectOutputStream(new FileOutputStream("fileIndex.ser"))) {
			oos.writeObject(fileIndex);
			System.out.println("Index saved to fileIndex.ser");
		} catch (IOException e) {
			e.printStackTrace();
		}
	}

	public static Map<String, String> loadIndex() {
		try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream("fileIndex.ser"))) {
			return (Map<String, String>)ois.readObject();
		} catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
			return null;
		}
	}
}
```
In this updated code, we first attempt to load the serialized index from disk using the `loadIndex()` method. If the serialized index is not found or cannot be loaded, we create a new `HashMap` and index all the files in the C drive, using the `indexFiles()` method, and then serialize and save the `HashMap` to disk using the `saveIndex()` method.

The `saveIndex()` method writes the `Map` object to a file named `fileIndex.ser` using an `ObjectOutputStream`. The `loadIndex()` method reads the serialized `Map` object from the file using an `ObjectInputStream` and returns it as a `Map<String, String>`. Note that we wrap both streams in try-with-resources statements to ensure they are properly closed when we're done with them.

This updated code allows you to perform updates to the index and persist them to disk for later use without having to re-index all the files on the C drive. Additionally, it adds error handling to handle any issues encountered during serialization.

