```
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>

using namespace std;

void timer(int seconds) {
    cout << "Timer started for " << seconds << " seconds." << endl;
    for (int i = seconds; i >= 0; i--) {
        cout << i << " ";
        this_thread::sleep_for(chrono::seconds(1));
    }
    cout << "\nTime's up!" << endl;
}

int main() {
    timer(10); // start a 10-second timer
    return 0;
}
```

The `timer` function takes an integer parameter `seconds` which specifies the duration of the timer in seconds. The function displays a message indicating that the timer has started, then counts down from `seconds` to 0. Each count is displayed on the screen, and the function waits for 1 second between each count using the `this_thread::sleep_for` function from the `thread` library. When the timer reaches 0, the function displays a message indicating that the time is up.

In the main function, you can call `timer` with the desired duration to start the timer. Keep in mind that this implementation uses busy waiting, meaning that the function will use processing time even while it is waiting. If you need a more precise timer, you may need to use hardware interrupts or other mechanisms.

