If you want to flip the WebGL texture while uploading it from an HTML Image or Canvas, you can use the `gl.pixelStorei(gl.UNPACK_FLIP_Y_WEBGL, 1)` option, not `0`. This will flip the image vertically during the pixel transfer operation. Here's an example:

```javascript
const texture = gl.createTexture();
gl.bindTexture(gl.TEXTURE_2D, texture);
gl.pixelStorei(gl.UNPACK_FLIP_Y_WEBGL, 1); // Set the flag to flip the y-axis

// Upload the image to the texture
const level = 0;
const internalFormat = gl.RGBA;
const format = gl.RGBA;
const type = gl.UNSIGNED_BYTE;
gl.texImage2D(gl.TEXTURE_2D, level, internalFormat, format, type, image);

// Set other texture parameters like filtering and wrapping
gl.texParameteri(gl.TEXTURE_2D, gl.TEXTURE_MIN_FILTER, gl.LINEAR);
gl.texParameteri(gl.TEXTURE_2D, gl.TEXTURE_MAG_FILTER, gl.LINEAR);
gl.texParameteri(gl.TEXTURE_2D, gl.TEXTURE_WRAP_S, gl.CLAMP_TO_EDGE);
gl.texParameteri(gl.TEXTURE_2D, gl.TEXTURE_WRAP_T, gl.CLAMP_TO_EDGE);
```

Note that, in the code above, we've set `gl.UNPACK_FLIP_Y_WEBGL` to `1`. This option is useful when you want to flip the texture source (like an image, video or canvas) on the y-axis during the pixel transfer operation from the source to the WebGL texture.

If you want to revert back to not flipping the image, you can set the flag back to `0` like so:

```javascript
gl.pixelStorei(gl.UNPACK_FLIP_Y_WEBGL, 0);
```

Keep in mind, this only affects the texture uploads (pixel transfer operations) occurring after setting the flag. It does not modify the content of the framebuffer itself. However, if you render the aforementioned texture into the framebuffer, the rendered image will appear correctly (not flipped) as it takes care of the flipped y-axis during the uploading process to the texture.

