2. Define the routes and create components:

For simplicity, let's define two routes: a home page and a contact page. We'll create two components: `Home` and `Contact`.

```
import React from 'react';
import { BrowserRouter as Router, Switch, Route, Link } from "react-router-dom";

const Home = () => {
  return <h1>Welcome to the Home Page!</h1>;
};

const Contact = () => {
  return <h1>Get in touch with us at contact@example.com</h1>;
};

const App = () => {
  return (
    <Router>
      <div>
        <nav>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <Link to="/">Home</Link>
            </li>
            <li>
              <Link to="/contact">Contact</Link>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <Switch>
          <Route exact path="/">
            <Home />
          </Route>
          <Route path="/contact">
            <Contact />
          </Route>
        </Switch>
      </div>
    </Router>
  );
};

export default App;
```

3. Handle user interactions:

Let's say we want to handle a form submission. We can do this in the `Contact` component:

```
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const Contact = () => {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
  const [message, setMessage] = useState('');

  const handleSubmit = (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    console.log(`Name: ${name}\nEmail: ${email}\nMessage: ${message}`);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Get in touch with us</h1>

      <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
        <label htmlFor="name">Name:</label>
        <input type="text" id="name" onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} />

        <label htmlFor="email">Email:</label>
        <input type="email" id="email" onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)} />

        <label htmlFor="message">Message:</label>
        <textarea id="message" onChange={(e) => setMessage(e.target.value)}></textarea>

        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
      </form>
    </div>
  );
};
```

In this example, we're using the `useState` hook to keep track of the form input values. When the form is submitted, the `handleSubmit` function is called and the form data is logged to the console.

